{
  "name": "regex-annex-b-strict-unicode",
  "description": "Without `u`, Dart's RegExp reads ECMAScript's lenient Annex B grammar; with `u`, the strict one, and a pattern it refuses makes the `regex` transform resolve to null (the client catches the error). With `g`: an escaped letter with no meaning is the letter (`\\A` on \"aA\" gives \"a#\"); after a backslash p is the letter p and a brace that does not finish a quantifier is text (`\\p{L}` on \"p{L} a\" gives \"# a\"); `\\400` is the octal escape 40 (a space) then the digit 0 (\" 0\" gives \"#\"); `\\c1` is a backslash, c and 1 (\"\\c1\" gives \"#\"); a u escape without four hex digits is the letter u (`\\u{3}` on \"uuu u\" gives \"# u\"); `[^]` matches any unit, a line feed included (\"a\", U+000A, \"b\" gives \"###\"); `&` in a class is itself (`[a&&b]` on \"a&b\" gives \"###\"); a range with a class escape on one side is a union (`[a-\\d]` on \"a-1b\" gives \"###b\"); and a lookahead may take a quantifier (`(?=a)*` on \"ab\" gives \"#a#b#\"). With `gu` Dart refuses `[a-\\d]`, an escaped character that is not syntax (`\\-`), a lone `}` and `\\8` (no group 8), so these resolve to null, while `[\\-]` on \"-a\" gives \"#a\" and `\\cA` matches U+0001. A quantifier on a quantifier (`a++`) is null in both modes. `\\s+` on the 25 characters ECMAScript calls white space or a line end (tab to carriage return, the space, U+00A0, U+1680, U+2000 to U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F, U+3000, U+FEFF) followed by U+0085, U+180E, U+200B and \"a\" replaces the 25 as one run and keeps the rest: U+FEFF is white space, U+0085 is not. The empty class matches nothing, so `[]*` on \"ab\" gives \"#a#b#\". Every replace-mode regex puts \"#\" for each match. Every input reads page state, so the engine keeps the Values unresolved with `watches` and the client resolves them.",
